WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
Bear's Notes
Why a Dim Blue Lamp Beats a Blinding Red One

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:07.923
Aim the brightest red lamp you own at a metal plate — and not one electron escapes.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.603
Swap in a dim blue glow, and electrons fly off instantly.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.183
Crank the red to blinding and still — nothing.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.690
So stop picturing a smooth wave: light arrives as separate packets.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:24.440
Each packet's energy is set only by its color — red packets weak, blue packets strong.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.797
Brightness just means more packets per second, never fatter ones.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.037
And an electron breaks free only if one single packet pays its whole escape cost at once.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.953
Every red packet falls short — a flood of near-misses frees nothing.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.610
One faint blue packet clears the cost in a single hit, and the electron flies.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.973
That's E equals h nu: color is energy, and the packet is called a photon.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.503
Brightness counts the packets; color decides whether any one of them can pay.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:01:01.763
The full story — the leftover energy, the threshold color, the sodium numbers — is in the long video. Thanks for watching — more at youtube dot com, slash, at Nik Bear Brown.
